  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 955 PM MDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nISOLATED SHOWERS AND THUNDERSTORMS THROUGH THE WEEK:\n\nStorms Tuesday over-performed compared to what was seen in hi-\nres model guidance the past couple days. Though storms were in \nfact isolated as anticipated, they also proved capable of \ndropping small hail, producing bouts of heavy rainfall, and even\ndeveloping in valley locations. Satellite imagery and radar \nreveal one culprit for this being a MCV which has persisted over\nthe western San Juans, supporting more favorable dynamics for \nstronger thunderstorms. This paired with a few strong outflows \nto result in activity beyond what was expected.\n\nInterestingly, one of the only models to detect this MCV, the \nGFS, is also hinting at a similar feature developing tomorrow \nafternoon (though it is not as pronounced). Meanwhile, other \nmodels are not detecting this feature, also consistent with\ntrends leading up to Tuesday. We don't want to put all of our \neggs in one basket, but considering the GFS reasonably modeled \ntoday's overall pattern, we cannot discount similar weather \ntomorrow. This primarily means two things: storms may be \ncapable of producing strong winds, small hail, and localized \nheavy rainfall again; and some valleys (primarily along and \nsouth of I-70) may see measurable precipitation Wednesday in\naddition to the high terrain. NBM probabilities of measurable \nprecipitation in these valleys Wednesday is around 10-20%, but \ngiven the situation, it may be safest to treat that as the low-\nend scenario. As such, we have boosted PoP's over the southern \nhalf of the region by about 10% Wednesday. It is vital to note \nthat while we are messaging the potential for some more potent \nweather Wednesday, this is only expected to occur should a MCV \nredevelop Wednesday afternoon. Should the pattern follow most \nother models' guidance (i.e. no MCV), look for storms to be \nweaker and much more confined to the high terrain.\n\nOtherwise, Thursday and Friday look to be on track for mostly \nterrain-driven showers with minimal impacts. High temperatures\ncontinue to sit 5 to 15 degrees above normal as well.\n\nPOTENTIAL FOR WIDESPREAD RAINFALL RAMPS UP THROUGH THE WEEKEND:\n\nThe PacNW trough which we have been keeping an eye on seems to \nbe on the slightly faster track, meaning we should see \nprecipitation potential begin to ramp up beginning Saturday. \nRegardless, moisture and support won't be favorable for \nwidespread storms until Sunday and beyond. Both the GFS Ens and \nECMWF Ens are in agreement with PWAT's reaching their peak \n(225-250% of normal) around midnight to 6 AM local time Sunday \nand persisting at least through Monday. We remain too far out in\ntime to speculate just how long the pool of moisture will sit \nover us, but prepare for multiple days of rainy weather region-\nwide. As it stands, the wettest period appears on track to be \nthroughout the morning and afternoon of Monday, but this is \nstill a low-confidence detail of this system and the forecast \nstill has time to shift. The finer details of the storm may not \ncome into view until hi-res model guidance becomes available in \na few days, but we are confident that early next week will see a\nreturn of more widespread active weather.",
